#Up to this point, I had built a working deduction game and an agent capable of playing it.

The agent could:

- observe the remaining search space;
- choose a feature;
- select a value to ask about;
- receive a yes/no answer;
- eliminate candidates;
- occasionally make an early guess;
- and eventually identify a character.

But there was a problem with moving directly into Reinforcement Learning.

I had no idea what **good performance** actually looked like.

If I trained an RL agent and it solved the game in 18 steps, would that be good?

What if it solved it in 12?

What if a simple heuristic could do the same thing without any training at all?

Before I could evaluate whether an RL agent had learned anything useful, I needed something to compare it against.

## Why Build Baselines First?

My original agent was already heuristic-driven.

I had manually decided how it should think:

1. Look at the remaining candidates.
2. Analyse their feature distributions.
3. Choose something that appears useful to ask about.
4. Update the search space.
5. Repeat.

This gave me a working starting point, but it also created an important question.

If I eventually introduced RL, I did not want to simply say:

> The RL agent won, therefore it worked.

That would not tell me much.

The game could potentially be solved using a very simple strategy.

An RL agent might also perform worse than a basic heuristic.

So I started thinking about the project as a benchmark.

I wanted multiple agents facing the same environment.

The Baselines

I eventually defined four different non-RL strategies:

RandomAgent
CardinalityEntropyAgent
BinaryInfoGainAgent
EpsilonInfoGainAgent

They were not all intended to be equally strong.

That was the point.

Each one represented a different way of approaching the same decision problem.

Together, they gave me a spectrum ranging from random behaviour to increasingly informed decision-making.

1. Random Agent

The simplest baseline was the random agent.

It does not try to understand the structure of the search space.

It does not calculate entropy.

It does not calculate information gain.

It simply selects from the available actions according to its random policy.

This gave me a useful lower-bound reference.

If a supposedly intelligent strategy could not consistently outperform random decisions, something was probably wrong with either:

the strategy;
the environment;
the reward design;
or the experiment itself.

The random baseline was important because it established that not every improvement in performance should automatically be attributed to intelligence.

Sometimes an environment may simply be easy.

2. Cardinality Entropy

The next strategy grew out of my earlier work with entropy.

At this point, I was experimenting with how the number and distribution of possible values within a feature affected its usefulness.

A feature with more possible categories creates a different type of decision structure from a binary feature.

For example:

Sex
├── Male
└── Female

has a very different cardinality from:

Industry
├── Film
├── Politics
├── Music
├── Content
├── Entrepreneur
└── ...

The Cardinality Entropy Agent used the distributional structure of the features as part of its decision-making process.

This was useful as an intermediate heuristic because it gave me another perspective on the search problem.

However, as the project developed, I started realizing that scoring an entire feature was not necessarily the same as scoring the actual question the agent was about to ask.

That distinction would eventually become extremely important.

3. Binary Information Gain

The biggest conceptual change came when I started looking more directly at the actual action.

The agent does not ask:

Which feature is best?

At least, not in the final sense.

The agent ultimately takes an action like:

Is Hair color = blonde?

That is a binary decision.

The answer creates two possible partitions:

                     Current Candidates
                             │
              Is Hair color = blonde?
                       /             \
                    Yes               No
                     │                 │
            Matching candidates   All others

This made me rethink how the quality of a question should be measured.

The useful question was not simply:

Which feature has the highest entropy?

It was:

How effectively does this specific yes/no question divide the current search space?

The Ideal Split

Suppose there are 100 candidates remaining.

Consider two questions.

Question A
Yes → 50 candidates
No  → 50 candidates
Question B
Yes → 90 candidates
No  → 10 candidates

Question A creates a perfectly balanced split.

Regardless of the answer, the search space is cut in half.

Question B might occasionally remove 90 candidates, but most of the time it leaves 90 candidates behind.

For a decision strategy that wants to minimize uncertainty on average, these are not equivalent.

This led me toward the information-theoretic interpretation of the game.

So if a question consistently divides the search space into two equal halves, every answer gives exactly one bit of information.

This gave me a much clearer idea of what an efficient strategy should look like.

A strong question is one that creates the most balanced partition of the remaining candidates.

A Theoretical Reference Point

Once I started thinking about the game as a sequence of binary partitions, I could estimate the theoretical number of questions required and that number became a useful reference point for the experiments.

If an agent could consistently identify a character in roughly 12 questions, it was behaving close to the theoretical limit for balanced binary decisions.

This was one of the moments where the project changed for me.

I was no longer only watching whether the agent won.

I could now ask:

How far is its behaviour from the theoretical efficiency of the environment?

That is a much more interesting measurement than simple win rate.

4. Epsilon Information Gain

The final heuristic introduced some controlled randomness.

The idea was similar to the exploration mechanisms I had already experimented with in earlier versions of the agent.

Most of the time:

Choose the question with the strongest information-gain potential.

But occasionally:

Explore another available action.

Conceptually:

With probability ε
        │
        ▼
Explore a different action

With probability 1 - ε
        │
        ▼
Choose the best-known information-gain action

This gave me a heuristic somewhere between:

completely deterministic optimisation, and completely random behaviour.

This reflected on one of the most emphasized problems but Sutton and Barto in RL:

exploration versus exploitation.

At this stage, however, this was still a manually designed heuristic.

The agent was not learning from rewards.

I was explicitly defining:

what "good" meant;
how to measure it;
and when the agent should deliberately behave differently.
What the Heuristics Revealed

Once I started running the different agents against the same environment, the differences became much more visible.

The information-based heuristics consistently performed far better than uninformed strategies.

This was important for two reasons.

First, the game was not just about eliminating the largest number of candidates from one branch.

It was about reducing uncertainty efficiently across possible outcomes.

Second, it gave me a real benchmark for the next stage of the project.

I now knew that an RL agent should not merely learn to win.

A stronger question was:

Can an RL agent learn a strategy that approaches the behaviour of the information-based heuristics?

The Step Limit Became Part of the Experiment

I had also introduced a maximum number of steps:

max_steps = 20

Initially, this was simply a practical game constraint.

I did not want an agent to continue asking questions indefinitely.

But once I started comparing strategies, the step limit became part of the experiment itself.

A weak or poorly ordered strategy might continue reducing the search space but still fail to isolate the target within 20 turns.

A strong strategy could consistently finish much earlier.

This meant that performance was no longer just:

Win / Loss

It also involved:

How quickly?
How consistently?
How efficiently?

The metrics started becoming more meaningful:

win rate;
average number of steps;
candidate-space contraction;
final number of remaining candidates;
behaviour as the search space increased.

The game was gradually becoming an experimental environment.

A Change in How I Viewed the Project

Before building these baselines, my plan was roughly:

Build game
     ↓
Build RL agent
     ↓
Train it

But the heuristic experiments changed that.

The project became:

Build game
     ↓
Understand the decision problem
     ↓
Build simple strategies
     ↓
Measure their behaviour
     ↓
Establish strong baselines
     ↓
Understand what good performance looks like
     ↓
Then introduce RL

This was a much better foundation.

Without the baselines, an RL result would have existed in isolation.

With them, I had something to challenge it against.

The Question I Was Ready to Ask Next

By this point, I had:

a working environment;
multiple heuristic strategies;
a random baseline;
measurable performance metrics;
an information-theoretic reference point;
and a clearer understanding of what efficient play looked like.

I was finally ready to introduce Reinforcement Learning.

But the next step was not as straightforward as I expected.

My initial idea was simple:

Put every possible decision into one action space and let the RL agent learn what to do.

That sounded reasonable.

The environment had questions.

The environment had possible guesses.

Both were actions.

So I combined them.

I did not yet realize that this decision would create the first major RL failure in the project.

The next experiment would teach me something I had not fully appreciated until then:

An environment can be logically correct and still be extremely difficult for an RL agent to learn from.